    "knowledge_point_id": "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0",
    "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/d7a5a3f962080fe457e5d35a581ee1340f1603f0c3bc64be4de6e7553f4402e1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
    "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,-5),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y+5=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,7),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y+5=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t.\n$$",
